{"name":"Paradise Bird","hex":"#ff8c55","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"paradise bird","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Synonym of bird of paradise."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/paradise_bird"}],"entries":[{"title":"Paradise Bird","blocks":[{"text":"Paradise Bird is a studio album by Amii Stewart released in September 1979. The album yielded two European single releases, \"Jealousy\" and double A-side \"The Letter\" / \"Paradise Bird\". The album was not as successful in the USA."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/b8/Amii_Stewart_-_Paradise_Bird.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paradise_Bird","wikidata":"Q7134241"},{"title":"Paradise","blocks":[{"text":"In religion and folklore, paradise is a place of everlasting happiness, delight, and bliss. Paradisiacal notions are often laden with pastoral imagery, and may be cosmogonical, eschatological, or both, often contrasted with the miseries of human civilization: in paradise there is only peace, prosperity, and happiness. Paradise is a place of contentment, a land of luxury and fulfillment containing ever-lasting bliss and delight. Paradise is often described as a \"higher place\", the holiest place, in contrast to this world, or underworlds such as hell."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Angelico%2C_giudizio_universale_01.jpg/330px-Angelico%2C_giudizio_universale_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paradise","wikidata":"Q3363340"},{"title":"Bird","blocks":[{"text":"Birds are vertebrate animals constituting the class Aves, characterised by feathers, toothless beaked jaws, the laying of hard-shelled eggs, a high metabolic rate, a four-chambered heart, and a strong yet lightweight skeleton. They are warm-blooded, live worldwide, and range in size from the 5.5 cm (2.2 in) bee hummingbird to the 2.8 m common ostrich. There are over 11,000 living species of birds, classified in 44 orders. More than half of the world's bird species are passerine or \"perching\" birds. Nearly all birds have wings whose development varies according to the life style of each species. Wings, which are modified forelimbs, gave birds the ability to fly, although further evolution has led to the loss of flight in some birds, including ratites, penguins, and diverse endemic island species. The only known groups without wings are the extinct moa and elephant birds. Birds are the only extant members of Dinosauria, and are a type of paravian theropods. The digestive and respiratory systems of birds are uniquely adapted for flight. Some bird species of aquatic environments, particularly seabirds and some waterbirds, have further evolved for swimming. The study of birds is called ornithology."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Kleiber_Flug.jpg/330px-Kleiber_Flug.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bird","wikidata":"Q5113"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Gardens_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg/500px-Gardens_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Gardens_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg/1280px-Gardens_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Gardens_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg/500px-Gardens_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The luxurious palace and gardens of Neo-Assyrian king Ashurbanipal (ruled 668–631 BCE) at Nineveh, with original color reconstitution. Irrigation canals radiate from an aqueduct. The king appears under the porch. British Museum.[8][9]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGardens_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Jan_Bruegel_d._%C3%84._003.jpg/500px-Jan_Bruegel_d._%C3%84._003.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Jan_Bruegel_d._%C3%84._003.jpg/1280px-Jan_Bruegel_d._%C3%84._003.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Jan_Bruegel_d._%C3%84._003.jpg/500px-Jan_Bruegel_d._%C3%84._003.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Paradise by Jan Bruegel, circa 1620","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJan_Bruegel_d._%C3%84._003.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Nicolas_Poussin_-_Le_Printemps.jpg/500px-Nicolas_Poussin_-_Le_Printemps.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Nicolas_Poussin_-_Le_Printemps.jpg/1280px-Nicolas_Poussin_-_Le_Printemps.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Nicolas_Poussin_-_Le_Printemps.jpg/500px-Nicolas_Poussin_-_Le_Printemps.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Nicolas Poussin, Four seasons of paradise, 1660–1664","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANicolas_Poussin_-_Le_Printemps.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/%D0%9F%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%BD%D0%B8_%D0%90%D0%B2%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B0%D0%BC_%D0%B8_%D0%BF%D1%80a%D0%B2%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%BD%D0%B8_%28%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%98%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B8%29_%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%98%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BA_%D1%83_%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%98%D1%83%2C_%D0%B6%D0%B8%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%BF%D0%B8%D1%81